{"title":"Assigning people to tasks identified in email: The EPA dataset for addressee tagging for detected task intent","url":"https://aclanthology.org/W18-6104/","doi":"10.18653/v1/W18-6104","authors":["Revanth Rameshkumar","Peter Bailey","Abhishek Jha","Chris Quirk"],"year":2018,"abstract":"Introduces the Enron People Assignment dataset, in which tasks described in authentic workplace emails are associated with the person or people responsible for carrying them out. The paper identifies both tasks and responsible people, defines evaluation methods, and evaluates a model against baselines. This is direct evidence for action-specific owner or assignee extraction.","venue":"2018 EMNLP Workshop W-NUT"}
{"title":"eAssistant: Cognitive Assistance for Identification and Auto-Triage of Actionable Conversations","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3041021.3054147","doi":"10.1145/3041021.3054147","authors":["Hamid R. Motahari Nezhad","Kalpa Gunaratna","Juan M. Cappi"],"year":2017,"abstract":"Presents fine-grained identification of individual actions and associated metadata in productivity communication including email, chat, messaging, and social collaboration tools, evaluated on real-world datasets. Its request and promise representations associate participants with an action and allow an optional due date or time, making the work relevant to owner and deadline binding rather than merely actionable-message classification.","venue":"Proceedings of the 26th International Conference on World Wide Web Companion"}
{"title":"Lin: Unsupervised Extraction of Tasks from Textual Communication","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2020.coling-main.164/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2020.coling-main.164","authors":["Parth Diwanji","Hui Guo","Munindar Singh","Anup Kalia"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Studies extraction of specific tasks expressed through commitments and requests in emails and chat messages. Lin is an unsupervised approach based on dependency parsing and VerbNet and is evaluated across domains, including unseen domains. The work extracts task content rather than only predicting whether a message is actionable.","venue":"COLING 2020"}
{"title":"Fine-Grained Analysis of Team Collaborative Dialogue","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.05471","arxiv_id":"2312.05471","authors":["Ian Perera","Matthew Johnson","Carson Wilber"],"year":2023,"published":"2023-12-09","abstract":"Develops fine-grained dialogue analysis for authentic organizational Slack conversations in a software-development setting. The hierarchical annotation scheme includes collaborative acts such as proposals, requests, assignments, and rejections, and the work evaluates a transformer-plus-CRF architecture using long-range context. It is direct enterprise-chat evidence for distinguishing several action and decision-related communicative acts, although it is not a complete structured action benchmark."}
{"title":"Domain Adaptation for Commitment Detection in Email","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3289600.3290984","doi":"10.1145/3289600.3290984","authors":["Hosein Azarbonyad","Robert Sim","Ryen W. White"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Studies detection of commitments to future actions in email and demonstrates substantial domain bias when models are transferred between email corpora. It evaluates feature-level, sample-level, and representation-based domain adaptation for commitment detection. The target is genuine future-action commitment rather than generic modality or capability.","venue":"WSDM 2019"}
{"title":"Monitoring Commitments in People-Driven Service Engagements","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/SCC.2013.62","doi":"10.1109/SCC.2013.62","authors":["Anup K. Kalia","Hamid R. Motahari-Nezhad","Claudio Bartolini","Munindar P. Singh"],"year":2013,"abstract":"Models and detects commitments in real-world email and chat service engagements, including commitment creation as well as later delegation, completion, and cancellation. Evaluation uses the Enron email corpus and an HP IT-support chat dataset. The commitment representation captures who is responsible to whom for achieving what, with circumstances as part of the underlying semantics, although the paper does not establish separate field-level accuracy for all such arguments.","venue":"2013 IEEE International Conference on Services Computing"}
{"title":"A Corpus-Based Classification of Commitments in Business English","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-007-6250-3_8","doi":"10.1007/978-94-007-6250-3_8","authors":["Rachele De Felice"],"year":2013,"abstract":"Presents a corpus-based study of commitments in authentic Business English emails using a speech-act-annotated corpus. It distinguishes action, informational, and interactional commitments and analyzes their lexical and phraseological realizations. This provides direct workplace-email evidence about commitment categories, though not a joint structured argument extractor.","venue":"Yearbook of Corpus Linguistics and Pragmatics 2013"}
{"title":"Routine politeness in American and British English requests: use and non-use of please","url":"https://doi.org/10.1515/pr-2016-0027","doi":"10.1515/pr-2016-0027","authors":["M. Lynne Murphy","Rachele De Felice"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Uses two comparable workplace email corpora that were pragmatically annotated so request speech acts can be retrieved independently of their surface formulation. The study analyzes 675 requests from each corpus and examines linguistic properties including mood and modal-verb use. It supplies direct empirical request annotation in workplace email, but does not evaluate structured owner or deadline extraction.","venue":"Journal of Politeness Research"}
{"title":"Making requests at work: An examination of phrase frames in workplace email communication","url":"https://www.revistaiberica.org/index.php/iberica/article/view/863","doi":"10.17398/2340-2784.47.67","authors":["Detong Xia","Matt Kessler","Yudi Chen","Hye Pae"],"year":2024,"published":"2024-06-05","abstract":"Analyzes 1,148 authentic request emails from the Avocado Research Email Collection. The study examines five-word phrase frames across rhetorical moves in workplace request emails and provides empirical evidence about how requests are linguistically realized. It is relevant to request identification and annotation but does not itself provide structured action arguments.","venue":"Ibérica"}
{"title":"Context-Aware Intent Identification in Email Conversations","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3331184.3331260","doi":"10.1145/3331184.3331260","authors":["Wei Wang","Saghar Hosseini","Ahmed Hassan Awadallah","Paul N. Bennett","Chris Quirk"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Studies sentence-level intent identification in workplace email using a large publicly available enterprise email dataset and models message and conversational context. The intent taxonomy includes action-relevant categories rather than only broad topical classes, with requests and future-action intents among the studied phenomena. It remains intent classification rather than full argument extraction.","venue":"SIGIR 2019"}
{"title":"Learning with Weak Supervision for Email Intent Detection","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.13084","arxiv_id":"2005.13084","doi":"10.1145/3397271.3401121","authors":["Kai Shu","Subhabrata Mukherjee","Guoqing Zheng","Ahmed Hassan Awadallah","Milad Shokouhi","Susan T. Dumais"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Uses user interaction logs as weak supervision for detecting actionable intents in email, combined with clean annotated data and self-paced learning. Experiments cover multiple email intent-detection tasks, including request- and action-related intents. The work provides direct empirical email evidence for communicative intent detection but not structured owner, deadline, or condition arguments.","venue":"SIGIR 2020"}
{"title":"Coding Dialogs with the DAMSL Annotation Scheme","url":"https://www.cs.rochester.edu/research/speech/damsl/papers.html","authors":["Mark G. Core","James F. Allen"],"year":1997,"abstract":"Describes the DAMSL dialogue-act annotation scheme and reports its application to dialogue coding. Forward-looking functions include influencing the addressee's future action and committing the speaker to future action, while backward-looking functions include agreement and relations to antecedent discourse. This is foundational transfer evidence for distinguishing directives, commitments, and agreement rather than workplace-email evidence.","venue":"AAAI Fall Symposium on Communicative Action in Humans and Machines"}
{"title":"Dialogue Act Modeling for Automatic Tagging and Recognition of Conversational Speech","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/cs/0006023","arxiv_id":"cs/0006023","doi":"10.1162/089120100561737","authors":["Andreas Stolcke","Klaus Ries","Noah Coccaro","Elizabeth Shriberg","Rebecca Bates","Daniel Jurafsky","Paul Taylor","Rachel Martin","Carol Van Ess-Dykema","Marie Meteer"],"year":2000,"abstract":"Develops statistical dialogue-act tagging on 1,155 hand-labeled Switchboard telephone conversations, with labels including agreement and disagreement alongside questions and statements. The work combines lexical, prosodic, and dialogue-sequence evidence. It is transfer evidence for contextual dialogue-act modeling, not direct workplace communication evidence.","venue":"Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"The Berkeley FrameNet Project","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P98-1013/","doi":"10.3115/980845.980860","authors":["Collin F. Baker","Charles J. Fillmore","John B. Lowe"],"year":1998,"published":"1998-08-10","abstract":"Introduces FrameNet's corpus-based representation of semantic frames and frame elements, with annotated attestations linking semantic roles to their syntactic realizations. The approach provides transferable machinery for representing action participants, recipients, times, and other arguments. It does not by itself establish task ownership or workplace obligation semantics.","venue":"ACL/COLING 1998"}
{"title":"Automatic Labeling of Semantic Roles","url":"https://aclanthology.org/J02-3001/","doi":"10.1162/089120102760275983","authors":["Daniel Gildea","Daniel Jurafsky"],"year":2002,"abstract":"Develops automatic semantic-role labeling using FrameNet-style predicate-argument structures. The work is foundational transfer evidence for extracting predicate-specific participants and adjuncts from text. Semantic agents should not be equated with assigned workplace owners, but the argument-extraction methods are relevant to structured action representations.","venue":"Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"The Proposition Bank: An Annotated Corpus of Semantic Roles","url":"https://aclanthology.org/J05-1004/","doi":"10.1162/0891201053630264","authors":["Martha Palmer","Daniel Gildea","Paul Kingsbury"],"year":2005,"published":"2005-03-01","abstract":"Introduces PropBank, which adds predicate-argument semantic-role annotations to the Penn Treebank and supports automatic role labeling. Its explicit predicate-specific argument spans are useful transfer evidence for structured action extraction and evidence provenance. The roles describe event semantics rather than communicative responsibility.","venue":"Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"Multi-Predicate Semantic Role Labeling","url":"https://aclanthology.org/D14-1041/","doi":"10.3115/v1/D14-1041","authors":["Haitong Yang","Chengqing Zong"],"year":2014,"abstract":"Addresses semantic-role labeling when multiple predicates occur in the same sentence rather than treating predicates independently. The method models interactions among predicates and their arguments. This is transfer evidence for binding arguments to the correct action when several actions coexist, not direct workplace-owner extraction.","venue":"EMNLP 2014"}
{"title":"Semantic Role Labeling of Implicit Arguments for Nominal Predicates","url":"https://aclanthology.org/J12-4003/","doi":"10.1162/COLI_a_00110","authors":["Matthew Gerber","Joyce Y. Chai"],"year":2012,"abstract":"Introduces a manually annotated corpus of implicit arguments for ten NomBank predicates and trains a discriminative model to recover arguments absent from a predicate's local context. The work demonstrates discourse-sensitive recovery of omitted participants. It is transfer evidence for implicit-owner research, but its semantic arguments are not workplace assignees.","venue":"Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"Inducing Implicit Arguments from Comparable Texts: A Framework and Its Applications","url":"https://aclanthology.org/J15-4003/","doi":"10.1162/COLI_a_00236","authors":["Michael Roth","Anette Frank"],"year":2015,"abstract":"Develops a framework for inducing and resolving implicit predicate arguments by exploiting comparable texts. The approach addresses participants that are not overtly realized in the local predicate context. It is transfer evidence for recovering implicit actors or owners from broader discourse, while not directly modeling workplace responsibility.","venue":"Computational Linguistics"}
{"title":"Refining Implicit Argument Annotation for UCCA","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.12889","arxiv_id":"2005.12889","authors":["Ruixiang Cui","Daniel Hershcovich"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Proposes a fine-grained typology for implicit arguments and annotates a subset of the UCCA English Web Treebank with categories including deictic, generic, genre-based, type-identifiable, non-specific, and iterated-set arguments. The resource is useful transfer evidence for distinguishing different sources of omitted participants. It does not directly annotate workplace task ownership.","venue":"Second International Workshop on Designing Meaning Representations"}
{"title":"Trigger is Not Sufficient: Exploiting Frame-aware Knowledge for Implicit Event Argument Extraction","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.360/","doi":"10.18653/v1/2021.acl-long.360","authors":["Kaiwen Wei","Xian Sun","Zequn Zhang","Jingyuan Zhang","Guo Zhi","Li Jin"],"year":2021,"abstract":"Studies implicit event-argument extraction and proposes a frame-aware method that reasons from other arguments in the event frame rather than relying only on the event trigger. Curriculum knowledge distillation allows the final model to operate without oracle arguments. This is transfer evidence for context-dependent recovery of omitted action participants.","venue":"ACL-IJCNLP 2021"}
{"title":"Explicit, Implicit, and Scattered: Revisiting Event Extraction to Capture Complex Arguments","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.03594","arxiv_id":"2410.03594","doi":"10.18653/v1/2024.emnlp-main.673","authors":["Omar Sharif","Joseph Gatto","Madhusudan Basak","Sarah M. Preum"],"year":2024,"abstract":"Introduces DiscourseEE, an event-extraction dataset with explicit, implicit, and scattered event arguments, showing that important arguments may require contextual inference or evidence distributed across a document. The dataset contains 7,464 argument annotations from online health discourse. It is transfer evidence for implicit and non-contiguous action arguments rather than direct workplace communication evidence.","venue":"EMNLP 2024"}
{"title":"A Structured Learning Approach to Temporal Relation Extraction","url":"https://aclanthology.org/D17-1108/","doi":"10.18653/v1/D17-1108","authors":["Qiang Ning","Zhili Feng","Dan Roth"],"year":2017,"abstract":"Models temporal relations between events using structured learning so that dependencies among multiple temporal relations are considered jointly. The method addresses missing relations and improves over approaches that make independent pairwise decisions. It is transfer evidence for action-to-time reasoning, not direct deadline extraction from workplace messages.","venue":"EMNLP 2017"}
{"title":"A Multi-Axis Annotation Scheme for Event Temporal Relations","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P18-1122/","doi":"10.18653/v1/P18-1122","authors":["Qiang Ning","Hao Wu","Dan Roth"],"year":2018,"abstract":"Proposes a multi-axis annotation scheme for temporal relations among events and refines the task by focusing temporal comparison on event start points. The work reports an expert annotation study and is aimed at improving consistency of event-time structure. It is transfer evidence for explicit temporal linking rather than a workplace deadline benchmark.","venue":"ACL 2018"}
{"title":"Joint Reasoning for Temporal and Causal Relations","url":"https://aclanthology.org/P18-1212/","doi":"10.18653/v1/P18-1212","authors":["Qiang Ning","Zhili Feng","Hao Wu","Dan Roth"],"year":2018,"abstract":"Jointly extracts temporal and causal relations using constrained inference, exploiting logical interactions between the two relation types. The formulation enforces global consistency rather than making isolated relation predictions. It is transfer evidence for linking temporal expressions or events to particular actions in structured extraction.","venue":"ACL 2018"}
{"title":"Fine-Grained Temporal Relation Extraction","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.01390","arxiv_id":"1902.01390","doi":"10.18653/v1/P19-1280","authors":["Siddharth Vashishtha","Benjamin Van Durme","Aaron Steven White"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Introduces a fine-grained semantic framework that maps event pairs to real-valued temporal relations and durations and uses it to annotate the Universal Dependencies English Web Treebank. Models jointly predict temporal relations and event durations. This is transfer evidence for richer action-time binding beyond standalone TIMEX detection.","venue":"ACL 2019"}
{"title":"Deep Structured Neural Network for Event Temporal Relation Extraction","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.10094","arxiv_id":"1909.10094","doi":"10.18653/v1/K19-1062","authors":["Rujun Han","I-Hung Hsu","Mu Yang","Aram Galstyan","Ralph Weischedel","Nanyun Peng"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Combines a neural pairwise temporal-relation scorer with structured inference enforcing constraints such as temporal transitivity. It is evaluated on multiple event temporal-relation datasets. The work is useful transfer evidence for maintaining consistent action-time relations but does not distinguish task deadlines in workplace communication.","venue":"CoNLL 2019"}
{"title":"A Modality Lexicon and its use in Automatic Tagging","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1410.4868","arxiv_id":"1410.4868","authors":["Kathryn Baker","Michael Bloodgood","Bonnie J. Dorr","Nathaniel W. Filardo","Lori Levin","Christine Piatko"],"year":2010,"abstract":"Defines a modality annotation scheme centered on three linked components: modality trigger, target, and holder, and develops a modality lexicon plus automatic taggers. The explicit trigger-to-target representation is relevant to attaching modal meaning to the correct action rather than assigning only a sentence-level modality label. It is transfer evidence and does not specifically establish workplace commitment extraction.","venue":"LREC 2010"}
{"title":"The BioScope corpus: biomedical texts annotated for uncertainty, negation and their scopes","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/1471-2105-9-S11-S9","doi":"10.1186/1471-2105-9-S11-S9","authors":["Veronika Vincze","György Szarvas","Richárd Farkas","György Móra","János Csirik"],"year":2008,"published":"2008-11-19","abstract":"Introduces a corpus with token-level annotations for negation and uncertainty cues and sentence-level annotations of their linguistic scopes. Independent annotators and adjudication were used to construct the resource. This is transfer evidence for linking negation or uncertainty cues to the textual material they govern, not direct evidence for negated workplace obligations.","venue":"BMC Bioinformatics"}
{"title":"FactBank: a corpus annotated with event factuality","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10579-009-9089-9","doi":"10.1007/s10579-009-9089-9","authors":["Roser Saurí","James Pustejovsky"],"year":2009,"abstract":"Presents FactBank, a corpus layered over TimeBank with annotations describing the factuality of events as conveyed by discourse. The representation captures distinctions between actual, non-occurring, and uncertain events arising from interacting linguistic markers. It is transfer evidence for event-level modality and negation attachment, not workplace deontic obligation extraction.","venue":"Language Resources and Evaluation"}
{"title":"A general framework for information extraction using dynamic span graphs","url":"https://aclanthology.org/N19-1308/","doi":"10.18653/v1/N19-1308","authors":["Yi Luan","Dave Wadden","Luheng He","Amy Shah","Mari Ostendorf","Hannaneh Hajishirzi"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Introduces an information-extraction framework that represents candidate textual spans as nodes and dynamically propagates information through predicted relation and coreference links. The approach jointly supports multiple span-oriented IE tasks and handles nested spans. It is transfer evidence for evidence-span-preserving structured extraction and cross-span argument relations.","venue":"NAACL-HLT 2019"}
{"title":"Entity, Relation, and Event Extraction with Contextualized Span Representations","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.03546","arxiv_id":"1909.03546","doi":"10.18653/v1/D19-1585","authors":["David Wadden","Ulme Wennberg","Yi Luan","Hannaneh Hajishirzi"],"year":2019,"abstract":"Presents DyGIE++, a unified span-based framework for entity, relation, and event extraction that scores text spans while incorporating local and cross-sentence context. Dynamic span graphs and coreference links support longer-range argument interactions. It is transfer evidence for retaining textual evidence spans while building structured records.","venue":"EMNLP-IJCNLP 2019"}
{"title":"Span-Based Joint Entity and Relation Extraction with Transformer Pre-Training","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.07755","arxiv_id":"1909.07755","doi":"10.3233/FAIA200321","authors":["Markus Eberts","Adrian Ulges"],"year":2020,"published":"2020-09-18","abstract":"Introduces SpERT, which searches over textual spans to jointly recognize entities and classify relations between them using transformer representations. Its explicit span-and-relation formulation provides transferable machinery for source-span provenance and argument binding. The evaluated tasks are generic entity and relation extraction rather than workplace actions.","venue":"ECAI 2020"}
{"title":"Event Extraction by Answering (Almost) Natural Questions","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.13625","arxiv_id":"2004.13625","doi":"10.18653/v1/2020.emnlp-main.49","authors":["Xinya Du","Claire Cardie"],"year":2020,"abstract":"Reformulates event argument extraction as extractive question answering, directly selecting argument spans for roles without requiring a separate entity-recognition stage. The method also evaluates extraction of roles unseen during training. It is transfer evidence for role-specific evidence spans and structured argument extraction rather than direct workplace action semantics.","venue":"EMNLP 2020"}
{"title":"Prompt for Extraction? PAIE: Prompting Argument Interaction for Event Argument Extraction","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.12109","arxiv_id":"2202.12109","doi":"10.18653/v1/2022.acl-long.466","authors":["Yubo Ma","Zehao Wang","Yixin Cao","Mukai Li","Meiqi Chen","Kun Wang","Jing Shao"],"year":2022,"abstract":"Presents an extractive event-argument model with role-specific span selectors and multi-role prompts so interactions among arguments are modeled jointly. Bipartite matching supports optimal span assignment, and the method can extract multiple arguments sharing the same role at sentence and document level. It is strong transfer evidence for action-specific span binding when several arguments coexist, but it is not a workplace obligation benchmark.","venue":"ACL 2022"}
===END llm_candidates.jsonl===
